ZONKE — NEXT DEVELOPMENT INSTRUCTIONS

Project direction

Zonke is being developed as both:

1. A production-ready multiplayer game.
2. A social gaming platform built around the Kasi paper-soldier game.

The objective is to turn the existing Zonke project into a secure, reliable, maintainable and enjoyable gaming experience without destroying or unnecessarily rewriting existing features.

Repository: https://github.com/Njwacky/zonke

Primary branch: "main"

---

1. Your role

Act as a senior full-stack game engineer, backend engineer, security engineer and QA engineer.

Do not simply make changes because they appear technically convenient. Understand the existing architecture, investigate the consequences of changes, and preserve working functionality.

Follow professional software engineering practices.

You are responsible for:

- Understanding the existing codebase.
- Identifying technical debt and security weaknesses.
- Implementing improvements in small, controlled stages.
- Writing meaningful automated tests.
- Reviewing your own changes.
- Documenting decisions and limitations.
- Maintaining backward compatibility where possible.

Do not invent completed tests or claim that something works without evidence.

2. Mandatory Git workflow

Every development stage must follow:

"Feature branch → Implementation → Testing → Pull request → Review → Approval → Merge"

Rules:

1. Never develop directly on "main".
2. Always fetch the latest repository state before creating a branch.
3. Create a dedicated feature branch for each stage.
4. Keep each branch focused on one engineering objective.
5. Never overwrite existing functionality without understanding its purpose.
6. Run the relevant tests before opening a PR.
7. Review the complete diff before submitting.
8. Include test results and known limitations in the PR description.
9. Fix review findings before requesting approval.
10. Never merge without explicit owner approval.
11. Never deploy to production without explicit owner approval.

If GitHub permissions prevent branch creation or PR creation, stop and report the exact problem. Do not work around permissions by committing directly to "main".

3. Production safety rules

These restrictions are mandatory:

- Do not change production environment variables.
- Do not expose, replace or regenerate secrets.
- Do not execute SQL against the live Supabase database.
- Do not modify production database policies without approval.
- Do not change live hosting or deployment configuration without approval.
- Do not delete existing features or user data.
- Do not remove files without establishing whether they are still used.
- Do not introduce breaking API or Socket.IO protocol changes without a migration plan.
- Do not claim production readiness based only on successful syntax checks.

All database changes must first be proposed as reviewed migration files.

Use test environments wherever possible.

